{"schemaVersion":1,"key":"fundamentals/multi-agent","track":"everyone","lang":"en","dir":"ltr","title":"Multi-Agent Systems","objectives":{"lang":"en","items":["Explain why splitting work between agents can help, using a team at work as the picture","Tell a supervisor (a manager) apart from a network (an open office)","Name the two problems every team of agents has: coordination and handover"]},"prerequisites":[{"key":"fundamentals/agents","number":6,"title":"AI Agents","drawn":true,"track":"everyone","lang":"en"}],"previous":{"key":"fundamentals/agents","number":6,"title":"AI Agents","drawn":true,"track":"everyone","lang":"en"},"next":{"key":"fundamentals/observability","number":8,"title":"Observability","drawn":true,"track":"everyone","lang":"en"},"toc":[{"id":"why-this-matters-to-you","text":"Why This Matters to You","depth":2},{"id":"why-we-might-need-multi-agents","text":"Why We Might Need Multi-agents","depth":2},{"id":"basic-multi-agent-architectures","text":"Basic Multi-agent Architectures","depth":2},{"id":"multi-agent-drawbacks","text":"Multi-agent Drawbacks","depth":2},{"id":"shared-context-or-isolated-context","text":"Shared Context, or Isolated Context","depth":3},{"id":"advanced-multi-agent-architectures","text":"Advanced Multi-agent Architectures","depth":2},{"id":"when-a-single-agent-might-be-better","text":"When a Single Agent Might be Better","depth":2},{"id":"words-you-now-know","text":"Words You Now Know","depth":2},{"id":"summary","text":"Summary","depth":2},{"id":"references","text":"References","depth":2},{"id":"-articles","text":"📄 Articles","depth":3}],"body":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"module","key":"fundamentals/agents","track":"everyone","lang":"en"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"AI Agents"}]},{"type":"text","text":" ended with one agent: one loop, one set of instructions, one set of tools. This module is about using several agents together. It works very much like people working together, and it brings the same problems."}],"lead":true},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"why-this-matters-to-you","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Why This Matters to You"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"You already know how teams work. A müdür (a manager) hands out tasks. A hoca (a teacher) teaches several students, and each one works on their own part. Colleagues hand work to each other, and sometimes something is lost on the way. A team of AI agents behaves the same way. So you already understand most of this module. It only needs new names."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"why-we-might-need-multi-agents","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Why We Might Need Multi-agents"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Imagine asking one person to be both the company's accountant and its graphic designer. They can do both. But their head is full of two jobs, and they make more mistakes in each."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"A single agent has the same problem. Take this request: "},{"type":"emphasis","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"\"How many sales did we make in each region? Show them in a bar chart.\""}]},{"type":"text","text":" That is two different jobs: first get the numbers from the database, then draw a chart. One agent can do both. But its instructions must explain databases "},{"type":"emphasis","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"and"}]},{"type":"text","text":" charts, and its tool list gets longer. So it has more ways to pick the wrong tool, and more room to mix up the two jobs."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"If you split the work, each agent gets short instructions, a few tools and one job, like two specialists:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"a "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"data agent"}]},{"type":"text","text":" that gets the numbers from the database"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"a "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"chart agent"}]},{"type":"text","text":" that turns the numbers into a chart"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Each one is an ordinary agent from "},{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"module","key":"fundamentals/agents","track":"everyone","lang":"en"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"AI Agents"}]},{"type":"text","text":". Nothing new was invented. You went from one loop to several."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"basic-multi-agent-architectures","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Basic Multi-agent Architectures"}]},{"type":"image","number":"7.1","title":"Single agent, network and supervisor architectures","note":"Left: one agent with its tools. Middle: a network, where every agent can talk to every other agent. Right: a supervisor, who receives the request and hands work to the others. The others talk only to the supervisor.","src":"course-images/fundamentals/multiagents.png","alt":"Single agent, network and supervisor architectures","width":4382,"height":1499},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Supervisor: a müdür and the team."}]},{"type":"text","text":" One agent receives the request, like a manager. It decides who should do each part, hands the work over, collects the results and answers the user. The team members talk to the manager and to no one else. It is like a hoca who gives each öğrenci (student) a part of the project and then puts the parts together."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"In our example, the supervisor sends the question to the data agent and gets the numbers back. Then it hands the numbers to the chart agent and returns the chart."}]},{"type":"diagram","number":"7.2","title":"Basic Multi-agent Architectures","svg":{"light":"api/v1/diagrams/b39cbcb047de00ea5a88686c9977b317.svg","dark":"api/v1/diagrams/ee43498c1202acfc5fc06f25b0397a93.svg"}},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"You will also hear this called "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"manager-worker"}]},{"type":"text","text":" or "},{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"orchestrator-worker"}]},{"type":"text","text":". It is the same shape."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Network: an open office."}]},{"type":"text","text":" There is no manager. Every agent can pass work to any other agent, and they decide among themselves who does what next. It is flexible, like a small team that organises itself. But it is much harder to follow, because no one place shows what was decided."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Start with a supervisor."}]},{"type":"text","text":" It is easier to check, because every decision passes through one place, the same way a manager knows the status of every task."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"multi-agent-drawbacks","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Multi-agent Drawbacks"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Both problems come "},{"type":"emphasis","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"from"}]},{"type":"text","text":" splitting the work. A single agent has neither. And both will sound familiar from any office."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Coordination."}]},{"type":"text","text":" Who does what, and in what order? How does anyone know a task is finished? Two agents can do the same work twice. One agent can wait forever for something that never arrives. A manager can keep handing out tasks and never stop. These are the same mistakes a badly run team makes."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Handover."}]},{"type":"text","text":" Each agent has its own chat, its own working memory. So when the data agent finishes, what exactly does the chart agent get? Only the numbers? The original question too? If you pass too little, the next agent works without information it needs, like a colleague who took over a project with no notes. If you pass everything, it costs a lot, and it brings back the confusion you split the work to avoid."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Getting the handover wrong is the usual reason a team of agents does "},{"type":"emphasis","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"worse"}]},{"type":"text","text":" than the one agent it replaced."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"shared-context-or-isolated-context","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Shared Context, or Isolated Context"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"This leads to one choice under every team of agents:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Shared context:"}]},{"type":"text","text":" everyone works at one shared desk and sees everything. Nothing is lost in the handover, but the desk fills up fast."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Isolated context:"}]},{"type":"text","text":" each agent has its own desk and sees only what it was handed. This is cheap and focused, but whatever nobody passed along is gone."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"advanced-multi-agent-architectures","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Advanced Multi-agent Architectures"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"These names will come up later in the course:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Hierarchical:"}]},{"type":"text","text":" managers of managers, like a company with departments."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Agent-as-a-tool:"}]},{"type":"text","text":" one agent is given to another as if it were an ordinary tool."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Subagents:"}]},{"type":"text","text":" an agent sends short-lived helpers to do one task each, like sending an intern to find something out. The helper comes back with only the result."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"when-a-single-agent-might-be-better","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"When a Single Agent Might be Better"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"More agents is not an upgrade. It is a trade. You get shorter instructions and clearer jobs. You pay with coordination and handover. Stay with one agent while one set of instructions still fits well. Split when one set of instructions has to cover two unrelated jobs, and split where the least information has to pass from one agent to the next."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"words-you-now-know","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Words You Now Know"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Multi-agent system"}]},{"type":"text","text":": several agents working together on one request."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Supervisor"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (manager-worker, orchestrator-worker): one agent hands out work and collects it."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Network"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (swarm): agents pass work to each other with no manager."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Coordination"}]},{"type":"text","text":": deciding who does what, and when it is finished."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Handover"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (context transfer): deciding what each agent is given."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Shared and isolated context"}]},{"type":"text","text":": one desk for everyone, or a desk for each."}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Subagent"}]},{"type":"text","text":": a short-lived helper that returns only its result."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"summary","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Summary"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Several agents let each one keep short instructions and a single job, like specialists in a team. The supervisor shape, a manager who hands out and collects work, is the one to start with. You get the same two problems any team has: coordination and handover."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"That is the core of Fundamentals. You now have the whole picture:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"a model that only predicts text"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"a chat that is its desk"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"search to fill that desk"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"tools to act"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"memory"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"a loop that makes it an agent"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"a team when one agent is not enough"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"strong","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Quick check"}]},{"type":"text","text":": What are the two problems a team of agents has? And what is the difference between a supervisor and a network?"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"references","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"References"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"-articles","inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"📄 Articles"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/multi-agent"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"Multi-agent systems"}]},{"type":"text","text":": the team shapes, in more depth"}]}]},{"blocks":[{"type":"paragraph","inlines":[{"type":"link","target":{"kind":"external","url":"https://www.langchain.com/blog/what-is-an-agent"},"inlines":[{"type":"text","text":"What is an agent?"}]},{"type":"text","text":": worth reading again, because a supervisor is a decision about how much the AI decides"}]}]}]}],"draft":null,"quiz":null}